Phlimm  <icubed@deathtospamremovethisbigfoot.com> wrote:

>WTF is the Death Drop strategy and what does it have to do with puddings?

Puddings divide.  The divided Puddings will heal and further divide.
There's no upper bound on the number of Puddings that can spawn this
way.  Since they are large creatures, they can and will drop pretty
much anything when they die.  For every one that drops an item, there
are about a dozen that won't.  For every one that drops something nice
like a magic lamp or speed boots, you have to kill a few thousand.  But
the lesser items are pretty good too.  You'll get so many scrolls and
potions that you will have to scroll through pages (dozens or hundreds of
pages) of inventory lists.  They drop plenty of food, and they are a
worthy sacrifice.  

Now, using this strategy makes the game pretty insane, since you can
have just about anything you'd like to have, if you are patient enough
(even stuff like artifact weapons, puddings have given me both Brands,
for example).  If you want to try enchanting armor past +5, you
eventually get enough spare stuff to try it.  It really is a completely 
outrageous way to play.

Now, the thing is, it can be dangerous in a lot of ways, even if the
puddings can't hurt you.  Other bad things can happen to the character 
while he's swimming through the sea of P's.  It can be difficult to get
around if you need to, say, deal with a creature at some distance.
Also, all the same YASD opportunities that exist with any kind of altar
camping apply here, especially if you're going for a half million turns
or more.

Considering how much discussion there's been about the farming strategy, 
I fully expect the next version to put some kind of severe limits on the 
practice, probably turning it into a liability on balance.  So I've been
doing it on the alt.org server.

I had read about people doing the pudding farming thing, but until I
tried it myself, I didn't appreciate the scale of the haul you get from
it.  I mean, I've played games that gave surplus object piles, but
nothing like this.  

It's abusive, but, I'm OK with the practice: for one thing, it's
exploiting a dangerous aspect of the game (puddings are tough, and the
fact that they divide makes them tougher, until the character gets to
the point that he can deal with them.)

Also, before you can really safely try this, you've pretty much got to
have most of your ascension kit anyway.  Even at AC -49 they get several
HP hits now and then.  Since you're often surrounded by them, you could
be killed.  Basically, my character was ready for Hell before he was
really comfortable with farming, and by then, he didn't really need it.
He could have gone to the planes 100,000 turns ago, and even though
there are massive (and I do mean MASSIVE) piles of loot, the stuff he
takes to the planes will pretty much be the same stuff as any other
game.  So the farming practice really amounts to a late-middle-game
doddle, and one way to help a wishless and/or polyless character get 
the kind of items that you usually wish for. 

Seriously.  I have one container just for HoB's and Speed Boots, and a
sack of spent magic markers.  They haven't dropped a WoW yet, but there
were 2 magic lamps and a few hundred smoky !oEH, and I don't think
there's any reason they can't drop WoW's, except for the statistical
scarcity of them.

I've even got my figurine of a Ki-Rin and my saddle.  

Did I mention pudding farming gives obscene amounts of loot?

